    "html": "<div style=\"width: 816px; height: 1056px; box-sizing: border-box; background: #FFFFFF; color: #1b2230; font-family: 'Public Sans', sans-serif; display: flex; flex-direction: column\"><div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; padding: 22px 48px 10px; border-bottom: 3px solid #04246f\"><span style=\"font-size: 11px; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: .16em; text-transform: uppercase; color: #04246f\">Fall into Motion · Patient Newsletter</span><span style=\"font-size: 11px; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: .16em; text-transform: uppercase; color: #c80e13\">November 2026</span></div><main style=\"flex: 1; min-height: 0; padding: 18px 48px 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 12px\">\n<p style=\"margin: 0; font-size: 11.5px; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: .16em; text-transform: uppercase; color: #c80e13\">Joint-pain spotlight</p>\n<h1 style=\"margin: 0; font-family: 'Newsreader', Georgia, serif; font-weight: 500; font-size: 38px; line-height: 1.08; letter-spacing: -.01em; color: #04246f\">What is pain keeping you from?</h1>\n<p style=\"margin: 0; font-family: 'Newsreader', Georgia, serif; font-style: italic; font-size: 18px; line-height: 1.4; color: #4a5363\">A conversation about treatment starts with the activities you want to keep doing.</p>\n<p style=\"margin: 0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.55\">Shorter walks. Interrupted sleep. Difficulty getting dressed or opening a jar. Bring those examples to your visit. They are more useful than simply saying, &ldquo;It still hurts.&rdquo;</p>\n<div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: 170px 1fr; gap: 20px; align-items: start; border-top: 1px solid #d5d9e0; padding-top: 10px\">\n<img src=\"assets/render-knee.png\" alt=\"Illustration of the knee joint with inflamed blood vessels highlighted in red\" style=\"width: 100%; height: 140px; object-fit: contain; object-position: 50% 50%; border-radius: 6px; background: transparent; display: block\">\n<div style=\"display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 7px; min-width: 0\"><h2 style=\"margin: 0; font-family: 'Newsreader', Georgia, serif; font-weight: 600; font-size: 20px; line-height: 1.2; color: #04246f\">Knee pain: Understanding GAE</h2><p style=\"margin: 0; font-size: 13px; line-height: 1.55\">Genicular artery embolization (GAE) is a minimally invasive option for selected people with knee pain caused by osteoarthritis. Using imaging and a thin tube called a catheter, a specialist treats abnormal small blood vessels associated with inflammation around the knee. The goal is to reduce pain and improve function without replacing the joint.</p><p style=\"margin: 0; font-size: 13px; line-height: 1.55\">An evaluation may be appropriate when treatments such as physical therapy, medication, or injections have not provided enough relief. Your diagnosis, imaging, medical history, and goals help determine whether GAE is a reasonable option. <b>GAE does not regrow cartilage or cure arthritis.</b> Some people still need joint replacement or other treatment, and results vary.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 170px; gap: 20px; align-items: start; border-top: 1px solid #d5d9e0; padding-top: 10px\">\n<div style=\"display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 7px; min-width: 0\"><h2 style=\"margin: 0; font-family: 'Newsreader', Georgia, serif; font-weight: 600; font-size: 20px; line-height: 1.2; color: #04246f\">Hip, shoulder, and hand pain: Understanding TAME</h2><p style=\"margin: 0; font-size: 13px; line-height: 1.55\">Transcatheter arterial microembolization (TAME) also uses imaging and a small catheter to target abnormal vessels associated with certain painful inflammatory conditions. Our team evaluates selected patients with persistent hip, shoulder, and hand symptoms.</p><p style=\"margin: 0; font-size: 13px; line-height: 1.55\">Tell us whether hip pain limits walking or sleep, shoulder pain affects reaching or dressing, or hand pain makes gripping and everyday tasks difficult. These symptoms are reasons to seek an evaluation, not proof that TAME is the right treatment. The evidence differs by condition and is still developing. Your consultation should address the likely cause of pain, what you have tried, potential benefits and risks, and alternatives.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 8px\"><img src=\"assets/render-shoulder.png\" alt=\"Illustration of the shoulder joint with the inflamed area highlighted in red\" style=\"width: 100%; height: 140px; object-fit: cover; object-position: 50% 18%; border-radius: 6px; background: transparent; display: block\"><img src=\"assets/render-hip.png\" alt=\"Illustration of the hip joint with inflamed blood vessels highlighted in red\" style=\"width: 100%; height: 140px; object-fit: cover; object-position: 45% 22%; border-radius: 6px; background: transparent; display: block\"></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 24px; align-items: start\"><div style=\"display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 7px; min-width: 0\"><h2 style=\"margin: 0; font-family: 'Newsreader', Georgia, serif; font-weight: 600; font-size: 20px; line-height: 1.2; color: #04246f\">A different approach: Peripheral nerve stimulation</h2><p style=\"margin: 0; font-size: 13px; line-height: 1.55\">Peripheral nerve stimulation (PNS) uses a small device to deliver mild electrical impulses near a targeted nerve to help manage certain types of persistent pain. Unlike embolization, it changes pain signaling rather than treating blood vessels. Suitability depends on the source and pattern of your pain.</p></div><div style=\"background: #04246f; color: #FFFFFF; border-left: 6px solid #c80e13; border-radius: 4px; padding: 14px 20px; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 6px\"><p style=\"margin: 0; font-size: 12px; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: .14em; text-transform: uppercase; color: #f6b3b5\">Your goal belongs in the conversation</p><p style=\"margin: 0; font-family: 'Newsreader', Georgia, serif; font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.4\">&ldquo;The activity I most want to get back to is ____________.&rdquo;</p><p style=\"margin: 0; font-size: 12.5px; line-height: 1.45; color: #d9e1f0\">Bring that answer to your appointment. Treatment decisions should reflect your life, not just a pain score.</p></div></div>\n<p style=\"margin: 0; font-size: 12.5px; line-height: 1.45; color: #1b2230; background: #f2f5fa; padding: 9px 14px; border-radius: 4px\"><b>Minimally invasive does not mean risk-free.</b> Ask about bleeding, infection, contrast-related risks where relevant, recovery instructions, and the possibility of limited or no relief.</p>\n<div style=\"flex: 1\"></div>\n<p style=\"margin: 0; font-size: 10.5px; line-height: 1.45; color: #4a5363\"><b>Sources and further reading:</b> UCLA Health: GAE · Society of Interventional Radiology: GAE position statement (2026) · HVLC: TAME · published hip, shoulder and hand studies · Stanford Medicine: peripheral nerve stimulation.</p>\n</main><footer style=\"padding: 12px 48px 22px; display: flex; justify-content: space-between; font-size: 11px; color: #4a5363; border-top: 1px solid #d5d9e0; margin: 0 48px; padding-left: 0; padding-right: 0\"><span>Heart Vascular &amp; Leg Center · Advanced Joint Pain &amp; Vascular Center · heartandleg.com · 661-324-4100</span><span style=\"font-weight: 700; color: #04246f\">3</span></footer></div>"
